# R11: Narrator lines for Wickermoor Village (region wickermoor). The Narrator is the Vagrant telling it afterwards: second person, present tense,
# one or two sentences, no game words (docs/design/narration.md section 2); his drawl is word choice and rhythm, a dropped g in about one line in three.
# Triggers: enter:<location id or area id> and examine:<prop id>. First draft by Claude; Gemini edits the batches that follow.

~ enter:wickermoor_village
| Timber-framed houses with steep shingled roofs crowd round a trodden square, every shutter closed and every doorstep lit by a carved turnip. The Silverbell mutters along the south edge like something that wants a word.
| Candle-glow leaks out of the cracks in the shutters of Wickermoor Village, and the mist off the moor smells of wet wool and woodsmoke. Somewhere a clock is keeping better time than anyone here.
once
~ enter:wm_round
| The Founder's Round is a hard-trodden clearing where five roads come in, a faceless statue on one side and a bare oak on the other. Folks walk round the oak and not through its shadow.
once
~ enter:wm_north
| Barber, tailor, bakery and constable stand shoulder to shoulder along the north side of the square, each with a turnip face grinning over its door.
once
~ enter:wm_moor_road
| The road north climbs onto open moor, a ditch beside it and a wind in it that carries the smell of far-off bonfire.
once
~ enter:wm_estate_hill
| The ground rises toward a manor with turrets on its hill, every window lit and every line of the place a little off true. Dead trees stand along its wall like folks waiting for a funeral to start.
once
~ enter:wm_west
| The west quarter keeps its schoolyard swing, its half-rebuilt church and its bookseller's narrow door, and the woods lean in close behind them all.
once
~ enter:wm_southwest
| Smith, apothecary, chandler and butcher share the lanes below the inn, and the smell is hot iron and tallow and blood, in that order.
once
~ enter:wm_east
| The east side is stone-heaps and log-piles and the sawdust smell of honest work, with the village's last houses straggling off toward a fenced lane.
once
~ enter:wm_south
| Gardens, plots and little graves lie along the south side, and beyond them the black glint of the river.
once
~ enter:wm_river
| The Silverbell runs dark and quiet under a footbridge, and the far bank is only woods and the road toward Maidenmist.
once
~ examine:wm_statue
| Ten feet of granite on a plinth, a figure with a cane and no face; the plaque says Our Village Founder and not one thing more.
| The founder's features have been worn smooth, by weather or by a great many thumbs, and you cannot tell which.
~ examine:wm_oak
| Hundreds of faces are carved into the oak's bark, mouths open, eyes shut, and every one of them is looking a little to the left of you.
| The bare branches hold no leaves and no birds. Where the roots break the ground they look wet, and it has not rained.
~ examine:wm_oak_roots
| The roots have lifted the cobbles round the oak into a small shivering hill, as if something under the square were breathing.
~ examine:wm_clocktower
| The clocktower rises out of the mayor's roof, its face as white as a boiled egg. It keeps perfect time, and now and then it keeps it at the wrong hour.
| The bell above the clock is bigger than the house under it, and some say it strikes of its own accord.
~ examine:wm_pavilion
| A little gazebo of weathered boards, with a bench round the inside and carved hares chasing one another along the rail.
~ examine:wm_inn_sign
| A painted Green Queen in a gown of leaves swings over the inn's door, her smile as warm as a priced ledger.
~ examine:wm_notice_board
| Bills for stray goats and tuber prices, and a handwritten line asking whoever has seen Bette Hollin to say so at the constable's. The line is a month old.
~ examine:wm_well
| The well wall is moss and old iron, and the rope has been replaced with a new one, still white at the ends.
~ examine:wm_bonfire
| A heap of bleached bones and driftwood at the village's edge, banked with ash, waiting for a night someone will name.
~ examine:wm_hare_effigy
| A hare woven out of wicker, taller than a child, one ear bent. Fresh flowers are laid at its feet, and nobody will tell you who laid them.
~ examine:wm_turnip_1
| A turnip carved with a long-nosed face, the candle in it burning low. Every house in the village has one, and each face is different.
~ examine:wm_turnip_2
| A turnip carved with a grin that is a good deal wider than a turnip has any right to.
~ examine:wm_stable_horse
| The horses shift in their stalls and watch you with their ears flat, as if they have heard of strangers.
~ examine:wm_cart
| A farm cart heaped with tubers, half of them with the black spot of blight, and a farmer's lunch left on the seat.
~ examine:wm_graves
| Small garden plots and smaller stone markers share the south edge, kept by somebody who has not given up on any of them.
~ examine:wm_pyethymer_gate
| A chain and a padlock hold the gate shut, and a board is nailed across it: BY ORDER OF THE MAYOR. Behind it a house leans over a lane of empty ones. On the step lies one small child's shoe.
~ examine:wm_sacrifice_doll
| A person-sized shape of sacking and straw, tied to a stake with good rope, one arm worked loose and hanging.
~ examine:wm_church_ruin
| Roofless stone walls with new timber framing climbing up inside them, and a fenced yard full of building stone. Songbirds roost in the bare rafters, and they do not sing.
~ examine:wm_man_in_marble
| An eight-foot knight in marble stands with his hands on a sword, head bowed in prayer, and the most sour scowl you have ever seen on a stone face.
~ enter:wickermoor_green_queen_inn
| The Green Queen Inn smells of woodsmoke, roast mutton and spilled cider. A stage the size of a wagon bed sits at one end, curtains drawn.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_green_queen_inn_upper
| A long upstairs hall with a door for every bed and a ledger of favours somewhere behind one of them.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_mayors_house
| Forms, in stacks and baskets and boxes. The mayor's office smells of ink, wax and cold tea, and the clock's tick comes down through the ceiling.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_clock_chamber
| Brass wheels turn in a cramped chamber, none of them in a hurry, and the whole room breathes with the pendulum.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_constable_station
| A cold stone office with three barred cells at the back, a kettle on the stove, and a notice board gone yellow.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_trading_post
| Shelves of staples and strange things from far valleys, and a smell of pipe smoke and lamp oil. The showroom is spotless, and so is the man who keeps it.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_apothecary
| Jars and drying bundles, a bubbling alembic and a table cleared for work. There is no bed anywhere in the place.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_barber_shop
| Two leather chairs, a basin, a still gurgling in the back, and the smell of hot lather and moonshine. A broom stands in a corner where somebody swept very carefully.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_schoolhouse
| Rows of small desks, a slate on a stand, and a hand-lettered map of the Hollow with a great many question marks on it.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_post_office
| Pigeonholes and parcels and a ceiling full of rafters, and one pigeon in them that does not look quite like a pigeon.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_dovecote
| Stacked cages and perches in a round stone room, and the air thick with feathers and the sound of wings.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_funeral_home
| Black marble underfoot and a hush like a held breath. A row of coffins waits against one wall, their lids polished like mirrors.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_bookstore
| Narrow aisles of old paper, a ladder, a pen-and-paper table in the corner, and the smell of leather and dust and candle.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_shop_bakery
| Warm as a held hand, and smelling of yeast and burnt sugar.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_shop_tailor
| Bolts of cloth in colours no one in Wickermoor wears, and a dress form dressed in something wonderful.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_shop_smithy
| Heat on your face and a clang in your teeth, and a quench-trough black as the river.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_shop_wickery
| Candles in every size hang in bunches like pale fruit, and the shop smells of beeswax and tallow.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_shop_butcher
| Cold, clean and not quite quiet; a hanging carcass swings a little in a draught from the pens.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_shop_masonry
| Dust and chip-chink, and a row of half-lettered gravestones waiting for names.
once
~ enter:wickermoor_shop_carpentry
| Sawdust, resin and a song in four-part harmony somewhere at the back.
once
